1790218951417265	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEymxU		item.comment	bn-1m952	{"body":"STOPPED, no commit. Emission at claim-A scope exceeds the wire limit: reference engine emit_finite_closure returns Oversized, 117,371,613 bytes > MAX_CERTIFICATE_BYTES 67,108,864 (64 MiB), same constant in kernel-core wire.rs:119. Measured: 22 vars, 46 actions, 61,504 states, 597,184 transitions; each transition writes its full 176-byte target state (106.3 MB of rows). Unblock needs a lead decision: raise the limit in kernel + emitter (kernel change, GOV4 two reviewers, C018 rerun) or a state-index transition encoding (wire epoch change). Also: kernel evaluates only PropertyClass::StateDomain, so the cert would not carry Agreement."}	blake3:3KoGAAf246bYtnCtcb1mrwTCJQlT-ro5yhb5dzQrGXM
1790218974258779	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEynBU		item.comment	bn-1m952	{"body":"Lead ruling after the worker's stop: re-scoped. At claim-A scope the certificate is 117 MB, over MAX_CERTIFICATE_BYTES (64 MiB), because each row carries a full target state. The kernel also evaluates only PropertyClass::StateDomain, so no register invariant would be certified. The deliverable is now a kernel design: certificate wire v2 with state-index transition targets (about 15 MB here, with a range check on each index), plus an invariant property class. It needs RFC text, a kernel change with two reviewers under GOV §4, and a C018 rerun. It is not on the PR-16 exit path (the mutant campaign), so it is deferred behind bn-5fpl and bn-28oa."}	blake3:9VdAToxVGVJ6cPIlK8MI94lI_58YkWVGLrPvX97ZoII
